ething that the  government would support so the the  backstop is is an integral part of the  withdraw all agree I know you're going  to have the Irish Minister on there to  be you have an opportunity to talk about  these things directly it is you know  that the backstop exists as a cast-iron  guarantee to not return to a hard border  between Northern Ireland and the  Republic and as I say it's been an  integral part of the withdrawal  agreement there are legitimate concerns  some of my colleagues and the Democratic  Unionist Party on aspect the backstop  and of course they do it would be  helpful and and it's already been  helpful actually to have some further  reassurances on that coming back from  the European Union but it's also worth  saying that there's no particular reason  to believe the backstop is going to come  into place anyway there's only an  insurance policies only yes and my  question was about will the government  support the backstopping time-limited  it's not it's not really a question  where the  government will support it or not this  isn't something which is entirely in the  gift of one side or other in the  negotiation it's an integral part of  their withdrawal agreement and any  negotiated exit' involves a withdrawal  agreement and any withdrawal agreement  would involve a backstop absolutely I  would support their being you know  additional reassurances if some were  possible on the way the backstop would  operate but we shouldn't and we  shouldn't over focus just on that aspect  because there are multiple reasons to  believe the backstop would never come  into place and even if it did actually  there's some advantages to the backstop  as well as as well as drawbacks what we  should be focusing on is the overall  package well the overall future  arrangement talking about the overall  package because made it pretty clear  this week that if the backstop is to  reweave visitors than the price of that  would be a permanent customs union can  you