ding people working in our  schools is fundamental to education and  we do have outstanding people working  our schools but we need to keep making  sure there's more and more of them we've  got about 450,000 teachers in this  country and that's about 10,000 up on  2010 but we've got we've had a growing  pupil population and we need to make  sure that the profession remains a  compelling attractive prospect and there  are some things which are working  against that like workload and that's  why I'm so focused on trying to bear  down on workload part of work they're  not the only part is email and I think  for all of us in all of our different  jobs there's been a bit of an explosion  in correspondence and communication  through email for teachers I think it  can happen even more so I'm not saying -  pear is never email the school or never  email pair because the teach because  that can be really important it's more a  question of you think about everything  else the teachers got on in terms of  lesson planning and preparation and  marking and so on and and and just a bit  of self-restraint when it comes to email  I mean I'm not an expert here but I  can't help but thinking if you want to  recruit more teachers and better  teachers you've got to pay them more and  actually if half years of pay restraint  for teachers and analysis by labor in  2417 found teachers a 5,000 a year worse  off compared to 2010 I mean that's the  figures are supported by the nu T where  all those years of squeezing teachers  pay a mistake pay clearly is an  important part of the attractiveness of  the profession as it is to other of  course to other walks of life and it is  possible you know through career  progression in teaching to build a build  a good career of course the teachers  pension is a very strong pensioners a  good part of that of that proposition of  that proposition as well but you know  it's also true that after the crash of  2008 there was I mean there was pay  restraint manifested in different ways  actually right across society not just  in the public sector and the private  sector as well but but we've just I mean  we've just had a larger pay increase for  for teachers ranging between one and a  half percent and three and a half  percent on the difference on the  different pay scales and I do of course  I totally accept that pay remains an  important part but workload is also very  important that's why I'm working to bear  down on that  also make sure that flexibility is  available in work so that when yet  perhaps for family reasons or other  reasons people wanna be able to work  part-time or in a job share to make sure  that those options are available okay  we're out of time thank you very much  thank you Sophie

